      "worked": "The line $2x-4y+1=0$ has gradient $\\frac12$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+1\\right)^2\\\\\\frac12\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+1\\right)^2&=\\frac12\\\\\\left(\\frac{x}{2}+1\\right)^2&=1\\\\\\frac{x}{2}+1&=1\\text{ or }-1\\\\x&=0\\text{ or }-4\\\\y(0)&=\\frac13\\\\y(-4)&=-\\frac13\\end{align*}$$\nAt $\\left(0,\\frac13\\right)$, $y-\\frac13=\\frac12x$, so $y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac13$. At $\\left(-4,-\\frac13\\right)$, $y+\\frac13=\\frac12(x+4)$, so $y=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac53$.",
